  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1013 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nPacific trough now over southern Nevada and continues to draw up \ntropical moisture, partially from Polo, over New Mexico. The trough \nis also drawing up tropical moisture from further west, but this is \nmostly over Arizona. 00Z KEPZ sounding shows PW around 1.5 inches, \nwhich is well above the daily record and sits around 250% of normal. \nSo between now and late Tue afternoon, the models show our highest \nPWs and likely our window for heaviest rain and flash flooding. \nCurrent radar is showing a bit of slack in the heavy rain, though \nstill raining decent over Dona Ana, Otero, El Paso and Hudspeth \nCounties. Both hi-res and larger scale models pick the heavy \nrainfall back up around 09Z-12Z Tuesday and continue into Tuesday \nevening. This corresponds with the time Polo reaches the Mexican \nmainland and then approaches our area. The heaviest rain looks to \nstart diminishing by around midnight Tuesday night, as the upper low \nsits over central Arizona and looks to bring some dry slotting into \nour area, at least briefly. The other items to watch for Tuesday \nare strong winds associated with the dying circulation of ex-Polo \nand severe weather. It looks like the remains will reach El Paso \nor west Texas late Tuesday afternoon. Will leave Wind Advisory out\nwith respect to area, though I think west Texas zones and \nsouthern Otero are most at risk for the winds. Will pare the \nending time back to midnight Tuesday night as the remnants quickly\nexit to the northeast. The advisory could blend into possible \nsevere thunderstorm warnings for the winds. \n\nWednesday...the tropical moisture is mostly flushed out to our east \n(PWs drop to around .5 to .7 inches) as the Pacific trough/low moves \nacross central New Mexico. Still expect isolated/scattered showers \nor a thunderstorm much of the day, but the heavy rain/flood threat \nshould be over. \n\nThursday through Saturday...Pacific trough now well to our east, but \nbecomes quite positively tilted. Models show a weak upper low \ncutting off and drifting back southwest over eastern New Mexico/west \nTexas. Thus a few showers could be possible over the eastern CWA \ntoward Saturday. \n\nSunday through Tuesday...the cut off low phases in somewhat with the \nouter circulation of Rachel, and forcing the upper high to northern \nNew Mexico. Thus some weak southerly flow develops along with some \nadvection of tropical moisture back up toward our area.\nBoth GFS/ECMWF show the moisture up into southern New Mexico Monday \nand Tuesday, though does not look to be a strong northward push of \nthe moisture at this time.",
